WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.980
File transfer is not only about bytes.

2
00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:10.440
Permissions, ownership, timestamps, modes, directory structure, and symbolic links can all affect the result.

3
00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:14.620
A file that arrives with the wrong mode may expose data or fail at runtime.

4
00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:19.280
A missing timestamp or ownership change may break downstream expectations.

5
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.080
Before automating a transfer, define which metadata matters.

6
00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:25.820
Some workflows only need file contents.

7
00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:31.280
Others need modes, timestamps, ownership, or directory structure preserved.

8
00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:39.940
rsync options can affect these details, so option choices should match the operational requirement instead of being copied from another script by habit.

9
00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:43.180
Sensitive files require special handling.

10
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:51.760
Transfer automation should avoid broad permissions, accidental publication, and routine logs that reveal sensitive paths or contents.

11
00:00:51.860 --> 00:01:00.020
If the destination is shared, staged, or consumed by another process, permissions and ownership should be part of the acceptance check.

12
00:01:00.300 --> 00:01:03.500
Symbolic links and link behavior deserve review.

13
00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:09.920
A script should know whether it is transferring a link, the link target, or a directory tree that may contain links.

14
00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:17.300
This matters most when automation processes shared directories, generated artifacts, or data prepared by another workflow.

15
00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:20.060
Directory boundaries should be checked before transfer.

16
00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:24.560
A source tree and destination tree should represent the intended scope.

17
00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:34.740
Post-transfer verification can confirm that important files arrived, expected attributes are present, and the destination did not receive broader data than intended.

18
00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:40.060
The secure habit is to verify the parts of file state that matter to the workflow.

19
00:01:40.160 --> 00:01:45.200
That may be a simple high-level check or a more formal release validation step.

20
00:01:45.300 --> 00:01:50.820
Either way, metadata and links are part of transfer behavior, not an afterthought.
